{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "append_file", "arguments": {"file_path": "orchestrations/agents/KB.md", "content": "## KB-008 -- 2026-06-09\n\n**Category:** backend\n**AgentRole:** Implement slugify() utility via Qwen/OpenRouter\n**Tags:** typescript, regex, utility\n**Trigger:** retry\n**StoryRef:** HW-006\n\nWhen implementing a `slugify` utility in TypeScript, a common gotcha is failing to chain `.trim()` *before* the regex replacement. If you replace non-alphanumeric characters with hyphens first, leading/trailing spaces become leading/trailing hyphens, which then require an extra cleanup step. The optimal sequence is: 1) `toLowerCase()`, 2) `trim()`, 3) `replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')` to collapse all non-alphanumeric runs into single hyphens, and 4) `replace(/^-|-$/g, '')` to strip any residual leading/trailing hyphens (e.g., from original trailing punctuation). This guarantees empty strings and whitespace-only strings safely resolve to `''` without throwing or producing `'-'`.\n"}}
